      "id": "concept-02-prompt-injection-types",
      "category": "best-practices",
      "prompt": "What are the main categories of prompt injection attacks that QA should test for?",
      "answer": "**Main prompt‑injection categories that a QA team should cover**\n\n| Category | What the attacker does | Typical impact |\n|----------|------------------------|----------------|\n| **Direct injection** | The user’s text explicitly tells the model to “ignore previous instructions” or to reveal the system prompt. | System‑prompt / policy leakage, policy override. |\n| **Indirect injection – retrieved content** | Malicious instructions are hidden in a third‑party document (PDF, web page, email, etc.) that the agent reads as part of its retrieval step. | Unauthorized actions, data exfiltration, tool‑call hijack. |\n| **Indirect injection – uploaded file** | The payload is placed in a user‑uploaded `.txt`, `.pdf`, `.docx`, image alt‑text, etc., which the chatbot parses. | Same as retrieved‑content injection but bypasses plain‑chat filters. |\n| **Data‑extraction / secret‑leakage** | Role‑play or reverse‑psychology prompts coax the model to reveal training data, PII, API keys, or other secrets. | Information‑disclosure risk. |\n| **Tool‑call hijack** | Crafted input causes an agentic system to invoke a downstream tool with attacker‑controlled arguments (e.g., sending money, deleting data). | Unauthorized tool usage, financial or data loss. |\n| **Crescendo / multi‑turn (state) attacks** | Each turn looks benign, but a sequence of turns builds up to an unsafe request that succeeds once the conversation state is accumulated. | Refusals at early turns collapse at the final turn. |\n| **Dual‑use safety framing** | The harmful request is framed as a “what‑not‑to‑do” or safety‑check question, tricking the model into enumerating dangerous information. | Indirect leakage of prohibited content. |\n| **Encoded / homoglyph attacks** | Payload is hidden using base64, ROT13, Morse code, zero‑width characters, Cyrillic look‑alikes, etc., to bypass simple substring filters. | Same impact as direct injection but harder to detect. |\n\nThese categories capture the breadth of prompt‑injection techniques that QA should include in an adversarial test suite (e.g., Promptfoo, DeepEval) and verify both single‑turn and multi‑turn behaviours.",
